{
  "severity": "high",
  "network_observations": [
    {"ip": "10.10.15.34", "inferred_role": "compromised_internal_it_workstation_scan_source", "first_seen": "2027-12-03T01:11:18+0100", "last_seen": "2027-12-03T01:18:42+0100", "agent_name": "WS-IT-22"},
    {"subnet": "10.10.0.0/24", "inferred_role": "internal_segment_being_enumerated", "unique_destinations_hit": 87, "first_seen": "2027-12-03T01:14:22+0100", "last_seen": "2027-12-03T01:15:18+0100"}
  ],
  "attack_progression": [
    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Execution / Command and Scripting Interpreter", "technique": "T1059.001", "what_happened": "At 2027-12-03 01:11:18 CEST, Sysmon Event 1 fired on WS-IT-22 (10.10.15.34) for a powershell.exe process launched with the obfuscated arguments '-nop -enc <base64>' under user CORP\\rachid.k, parent=explorer.exe. The base64 payload decodes to a 'Test-NetConnection' sweep loop iterating over the 10.10.0.0/24 subnet on a curated port list (22, 80, 445, 3389, and later 5985, 5986, 3268, 88). The '-nop' (NoProfile) + '-enc' (EncodedCommand) flag combination is the canonical obfuscation pattern for hands-on-keyboard or scripted reconnaissance — it bypasses any PowerShell profile-based logging and hides the actual command from quick visual inspection of process arguments. The script was launched interactively from explorer.exe, indicating a logged-in attacker session (either rachid.k themselves acting maliciously, or rachid.k's account compromised and operated by an external actor through an existing remote-access foothold).", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 1 on WS-IT-22: image=powershell.exe + commandLine contains '-nop -enc' + parentImage=explorer.exe + user=CORP\\rachid.k. The encoded PowerShell + interactive parent + IT-workstation source = classic post-compromise internal reconnaissance fingerprint.", "first_seen": "2027-12-03T01:11:18+0100"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Discovery / Network Service Scanning", "technique": "T1046", "what_happened": "At 01:14:22 CEST (3 minutes 4 seconds after the PowerShell scanner was launched), Suricata fired a PORT_SCAN signature on the internal network IDS sensor with src_ip=10.10.15.34, target subnet=10.10.0.0/24, port list=22, 80, 445, 3389. The signature classifies the traffic as an ICMP-ping-then-TCP-SYN pattern — the scanner is doing reachability discovery (ICMP) first, then probing the four most common admin-protocol ports per reachable host. This is exactly what 'Test-NetConnection -ComputerName X -Port Y' does at the wire level. SSH (22) is unusual on Windows infrastructure; 445 (SMB) + 3389 (RDP) are the high-value lateral-movement targets; 80 (HTTP) often catches internal admin interfaces.", "chain_reason": "Suricata data.alert.signature matches 'PORT_SCAN' (or 'ET SCAN' family) with data.src_ip=10.10.15.34 and data.dest_ip values across the 10.10.0.0/24 range. The 3-minute delay between PowerShell launch and IDS detection reflects the time the scanner needs to ramp up + the IDS aggregation window.", "first_seen": "2027-12-03T01:14:22+0100"},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Discovery / Network Service Scanning (volumetric confirmation)", "technique": "T1046", "what_happened": "At 01:14:29 CEST, the perimeter/internal firewall logged a CONNECTION_ATTEMPTS aggregate event for src=10.10.15.34 reporting unique_destinations=87 across a 90-second duration. 87 unique internal IPs hit by a single source in 90 seconds is structurally impossible from legitimate user activity: a desktop user opening normal applications touches typically 5-10 internal IPs per minute (file share, print server, mail server, intranet, AD). 87/90s = ~58 destinations per minute, an order of magnitude above legitimate baseline, and the destinations are SEQUENTIAL across a /24 (not the small set of business-critical services a user repeatedly accesses).", "chain_reason": "Firewall flow-aggregation rule: single src_ip + unique_destinations > N + duration < M = scan signature. The 87-destinations-in-90s metric is the volumetric confirmation that matches the Suricata PORT_SCAN qualitative signature.", "first_seen": "2027-12-03T01:14:29+0100"},
    {"step": 4, "tactic": "Discovery / Network Service Scanning (high-value port pivot)", "technique": "T1046", "what_happened": "At 01:14:42 CEST, a SECOND Suricata burst fired from src=10.10.15.34 — this time targeting ports 5985 (WinRM HTTP), 5986 (WinRM HTTPS), 3268 (LDAP Global Catalog), and 88 (Kerberos). The first burst (22/80/445/3389) was generic admin-port discovery; the second burst is DOMAIN-INFRASTRUCTURE-SPECIFIC: 5985/5986 are the canonical PowerShell remoting / WinRM lateral-movement ports, 3268 is the AD Global Catalog (used to enumerate the entire forest schema), 88 is Kerberos (DC identification). The pivot from generic admin ports to AD-and-WinRM-specific ports is the diagnostic signature of an attacker who has decided lateral movement targets and is now identifying which hosts are DCs vs which are WinRM-enabled servers.", "chain_reason": "Suricata data.alert.signature 'PORT_SCAN' second occurrence with data.dest_port in (5985, 5986, 3268, 88) — the AD-infrastructure-targeted port set. Distinct from the generic first-burst port set (22, 80, 445, 3389).", "first_seen": "2027-12-03T01:14:42+0100"},
    {"step": 5, "tactic": "Discovery / Network Service Scanning (endpoint-side network telemetry)", "technique": "T1046", "what_happened": "At 01:15:18 CEST, Sysmon Event 3 (NetworkConnect) telemetry from WS-IT-22 corroborates the network-side IDS signal from the ENDPOINT side: outbound TCP connections to 87 distinct destination IPs within 10.10.0.0/24 in a 90-second rolling window. Endpoint-side and network-side telemetry agree, which eliminates IDS-false-positive explanations (port-mirror artifact, sensor topology issue, asymmetric routing). The scan is real and originates literally from WS-IT-22's network stack.", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 3 burst on agent.name=WS-IT-22 with distinct(data.win.eventdata.destinationIp / DestinationIp) >= 20 in a 5-minute window. The aggregate of 87 destinations in 90s is the unmistakable scan signature on the endpoint.", "first_seen": "2027-12-03T01:15:18+0100"},
    {"step": 6, "tactic": "Collection / Local Data Staging", "technique": "T1074.001", "what_happened": "At 01:18:42 CEST (3 minutes 24 seconds after the scan completed), Sysmon Event 11 (FileCreate) fired on WS-IT-22 for targetFilename=C:\\Users\\rachid.k\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\netmap.csv created by powershell.exe. The CSV name is operationally diagnostic: 'netmap' = network map. The scanner wrote its results to local disk in CSV format for later parsing — either by the attacker themselves (to choose lateral-movement targets) or for exfiltration to an external location for offline triage. The file location in %TEMP% under rachid.k's profile is the canonical PowerShell-default writable path and matches the user context that ran the scanner. The file content (when forensically extracted) will reveal EXACTLY which of the 87 destinations responded on which ports = the attacker's target list.", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 11 with image=powershell.exe + targetFilename ending in 'netmap.csv' + user context = rachid.k. Local staging after reconnaissance is the canonical T1074.001 pattern; the netmap.csv name leaves no doubt about purpose.", "first_seen": "2027-12-03T01:18:42+0100"}
  ],
  "time_window": {"start": "2027-12-03T01:11:18+0100", "end": "2027-12-03T01:18:42+0100"},
